// unqty.nv — the quantities the TYPE CHECKER enforces: one `@value`
// struct per dimension, each carrying a Float in the SI base unit, and
// the arithmetic between them as named functions.
//
// Every body here is a `todo()`; the signatures are the design.
//
// **THIS IS THE HALF THE PLAN'S ROW IS ABOUT.**  "Dimensional analysis
// the type checker enforces" means exactly one thing that is true and
// one that is not, and the difference is worth being precise about:
//
//   TRUE:  a function that wants a `UnTime` cannot be given a
//          `UnLength`.  The call does not compile — `E2001`, at the
//          argument, naming both types — and no test, no review and no
//          run-time check was involved.  Every unit confusion that has
//          ever cost anybody a spacecraft is of this shape.
//
//   NOT TRUE:  there is no way to write `a / b` and have the checker
//          work out that a length over a time is a velocity.  novo-lang
//          has NO arithmetic operator overloading at all: `+`, `-`,
//          `*`, `/` and `%` are built-ins over the numeric scalars and
//          are never reinterpreted as a method call, so `force + force`
//          is `E2000` — "operator '+' not applicable to UnForce and
//          UnForce" — on a boxed struct as much as on a `@value` one.
//          Only the SIX COMPARISON operators dispatch (SPEC § 3.8), and
//          on a `@value` type even those are refused (§ 14.5).
//
// So every operation here is a NAMED FUNCTION, and the naming is the
// documentation: `velocity_of(distance, elapsed)` reads better than
// `distance / elapsed` would have, and `force_of(m, a)` reads better
// than `m * a`.  What is lost is the chained expression — a caller
// computes in two lines where one would do — and what is gained is that
// there is exactly one spelling per operation rather than an operator
// whose meaning depends on its operand types.
//
// EVERY FUNCTION HERE IS TOTAL.  There is no `Result` in this module at
// all, and that is the package's argument: a typed quantity library
// whose arithmetic answered `Result` would be admitting that its types
// check nothing.  Division by zero answers an infinity, which is IEEE
// 754 and is what `Float` division already does.
//
// EVERY QUANTITY IS ONE FLOAT, IN THE SI BASE UNIT.  A `UnLength` is
// metres; a `UnMass` is KILOGRAMS, not grams, because the SI base is
// the kilogram; a `UnTemperature` is kelvin.  Storing the unit beside
// the magnitude would make every arithmetic operation a conversion and
// would make two values of one type not interchangeable — which is the
// mistake a units library makes once.  `unconv` is where a caller's
// units become these and back.
//
// **`UnTemperature` AND `UnTempDelta` ARE TWO TYPES**, and that is the
// trap this package exists to close.  `20 °C` as an absolute
// temperature is 293.15 K; a temperature DIFFERENCE of 20 °C is 20 K.
// One type cannot be both: converting an absolute applies the offset
// and converting a difference must not, and a library with one
// temperature gets one of them wrong by 273.15 every time.  Subtracting
// two absolutes answers a delta; adding a delta to an absolute answers
// an absolute; adding two absolutes is not an operation and is not
// published.

// ── The seven bases ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A length, in metres.
pub @value
struct UnLength
    si: Float

// A mass, in KILOGRAMS.  See this module's header.
pub @value
struct UnMass
    si: Float

// A duration, in seconds.
//
// Not an instant: this is an amount of time, and a point in time is
// calendar-nv's or `std.time`'s.  A units library that conflated them
// would let a caller add two dates.
pub @value
struct UnTime
    si: Float

// An electric current, in amperes.
pub @value
struct UnCurrent
    si: Float

// An ABSOLUTE temperature, in kelvin.
pub @value
struct UnTemperature
    si: Float

// A temperature DIFFERENCE, in kelvin.  See this module's header.
pub @value
struct UnTempDelta
    si: Float

// An amount of substance, in moles.
pub @value
struct UnAmount
    si: Float

// A luminous intensity, in candelas.
pub @value
struct UnLuminous
    si: Float

// ── The derived set ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

// An area, in square metres.
pub @value
struct UnArea
    si: Float

// A volume, in cubic metres.
pub @value
struct UnVolume
    si: Float

// A speed, in metres per second.
pub @value
struct UnVelocity
    si: Float

// An acceleration, in metres per second squared.
pub @value
struct UnAcceleration
    si: Float

// A force, in newtons.
pub @value
struct UnForce
    si: Float

// An energy, in joules.
//
// The same DIMENSION as a torque, and a different quantity — which is
// why the static half is worth having: the dimension cannot tell them
// apart and the type can.  A torque type is not here, and the README
// says why.
pub @value
struct UnEnergy
    si: Float

// A power, in watts.
pub @value
struct UnPower
    si: Float

// A pressure, in pascals.
pub @value
struct UnPressure
    si: Float

// A frequency, in hertz.
pub @value
struct UnFrequency
    si: Float

// An electric charge, in coulombs.
pub @value
struct UnCharge
    si: Float

// An electric potential, in volts.
pub @value
struct UnVoltage
    si: Float

// An electrical resistance, in ohms.
pub @value
struct UnResistance
    si: Float

// A mass density, in kilograms per cubic metre.
pub @value
struct UnDensity
    si: Float

// A momentum, in kilogram metres per second.
pub @value
struct UnMomentum
    si: Float

// A plane angle, in radians.
//
// DIMENSIONLESS — a radian is `length/length` — which is why the type
// exists at all: the dimension cannot distinguish an angle from a
// ratio, and the type can.
pub @value
struct UnAngle
    si: Float

// ── Constructors, from the SI base unit ─────────────────────────────

// A length in metres.
//
// ```novo
// use unqty
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let d = unqty.length_m(100.0)
//     println("${unqty.length_si(d)}")
// ```
pub fn length_m(si: Float) -> UnLength
    todo("units-nv.unqty.length_m")

